# =============================================================================
#  What this repository publishes
# =============================================================================
#  COMMITTED : source code, methodology docs, and aggregate / pseudonymized
#              results (outputs/public/ plus the name-free result JSONs).
#  NEVER     : message content of any kind, real identities, and the large
#              third-party corpora. Rules are path-anchored to the current
#              layout — do not replace them with bare filenames.
#
#  See docs/ETHICS.md for the reasoning and docs/DATA.md for how to obtain the
#  corpora from their original sources.
# =============================================================================

# ── real identities — the one file mapping people to pipeline keys ───────────
config/team.json

# ── raw corpora: real people's messages, and far too large for git ───────────
# (Garimella alone is ~890 MB, above GitHub's 100 MB hard limit)
data/raw/

# ── parsed message streams: still verbatim message content ───────────────────
data/clean/

# ── feature matrices & labels keyed by real usernames ────────────────────────
# The Nankani rows carry real full names plus institution, and opus_labels.json
# pairs those names with behavioural judgements about the person.
data/features/
data/labels/

# ── annotation study: the shared apps embed real chat transcripts ────────────
outputs/annotation/sample_users.json
outputs/annotation/answer_key_DO_NOT_SHARE.json
outputs/annotation/annotator_*/
outputs/annotation/*.zip
# The returned label CSVs key their rows by the SOURCE-CORPUS username, and the
# Nankani ones are real full names — so the originals stay private too. Copies
# with opaque ids are also individual records and are not distributed.
outputs/annotation/returned/

# ── working results ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# outputs/results/ is the pipeline's private scratch space: some files name team
# members, and prose notes inside otherwise-aggregate files do too. Results reach
# the public repo through a selected-aggregate exporter and review. Keep this
# a whole-directory rule
# so a new result file is private by default rather than published by accident.
outputs/results/

# ── trained artifacts: large binaries, rebuildable from the pipeline ─────────
outputs/models/

# ── rendered deliverables: contain team members' names and per-person results
archive/generated_deliverables/
archive/old_results/
archive/opus_labels.backup.json
archive/*.zip
archive/*.ipynb
archive/kernel-metadata.json

# ── legacy top-level names (kept so older checkouts stay clean) ──────────────
database.sqlite
*.sqlite
slack_data/
slack_clean.json
slack_results.json
reddit_raw.*
reddit_clean.json
reddit_raw.csv
whatsapp_chat.txt
whatsapp_groupchat.txt
whatsapp_clean.json
whatsapp_results.json
whatsapp_features.json
wa_features.json
wa_results.json
nankani_clean.json
mbada_clean.json
training_results*.json
opus_labels.json

# Only reviewed aggregate filenames belong in the public directory.
outputs/public/*
!outputs/public/README.md
!outputs/public/ml_results.json
!outputs/public/annotation_agreement.json
!outputs/public/eval_vs_opus.json
!outputs/public/feature_weights.json
!outputs/public/feature_weights_perarch.json
outputs/annotation/
docs/assets/
docs/*.pdf
docs/*.pptx
docs/text_features_table.png

# ── Python / tooling ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# ── virtual environments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SNA/
venv/
env/
.venv/

# Credentials and local experiments
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
outputs/demo/
outputs/runs/
